  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure continues to build across northern New England \nover the next couple of days. This brings with it a seasonably \ncool airmass, as well as some chances for frost tonight and \nagain Wednesday night. A frost advisory has been issued for a \nfew zones that are still active through central New Hampshire \nand parts of western Maine. South of these areas, a \nnortheasterly breeze overnight keeps any frost isolated and \npatchy. Tomorrow night looks cooler as the high pressure center \npeaks overhead, so more areas of frost will be possible tomorrow\nnight as well. During the daytime, mainly sunny skies with \nhighs in the 60s continue.\n\nWhile the high brings cool and fair weather to northern New \nEngland, it also keeps a developing storm system off the \nCarolinas to our south. The surf will start to build on \nWednesday as a long easterly fetch develops across the North \nAtlantic between the high overhead and the developing storm \nsystem. These waves will steadily build on Thursday and through \nthe end of the week bringing increasingly large breaking surf \nand rip currents. Most land based impacts from the system hold \noff until at least this weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nStarting Friday...An area of low pressure offshore of the Mid-\nAtlantic is forecast to slowly lift northward and deepen Friday into \nthe first part of the weekend before stalling out and meandering for \nat least the second half of the weekend. There's slightly more \nagreement within today's ensembles in the northward extent the \nlow gets, but there continues to be a fair amount of spread in \nhow far offshore it remains or if there is a drift back west \nbefore pulling away early next week. The majority of the \nGEFS/AIGFS ensemble members are favoring the low center closer \nto but still south or east of Cape Cod while the majority of the\nECMWF camp favors the low a bit farther to the west...south of \nthe southern New England coast. It should be noted that each of \nthese models also has multiple members that support the other \nsolution as well as some farther offshore, so a degree of \ncertainty exists.\n\nOf higher confidence/less uncertainty: either way, the pressure \ngradient will be tight across the region between the low and a high \nto the north and west. So gusty north to northeast winds are \nincreasingly likely, especially along the coast. In addition to \nthis, a long fetch of these winds and swell will build seas over the \nGulf of Maine and bring the threat of high surf and a high risk of \nrip currents at area beaches. This will also coincide with a period \nof high astronomical tides, and given the increasing wind/wave \nforecast, coastal flooding will be another possibility surrounding \nthe times of high tide.\n\nLesser confidence and more uncertainty is how much rain will \nfall, but there is potential for soaking rain, especially along\nthe coast and portions of southern NH. NBM PoPs bring the \nhigher (low end likely) PoPs along the coast and into southeast \nNH which is sufficient for the time being given the remaining \nspread in the guidance. Temperatures may also stay in the 50s if\nthe rainier/cloudier scenario pans out.\n\nThe weekend conditions may carry into Monday, but ensembles \nfavor the low pulling away to the east by Tuesday of next week.",
